# Matchday hashes checked-in files byte-for-byte and freezes those hashes into
# governance policy: the MLB challenger artifact (mlb_challenger_store.py), the
# CFB challenger (build_cfb_challenger.py), and every manual-review evidence
# file a release review binds to (mlb_recovery.py). A hash taken over bytes is
# only stable if the bytes are.
#
# Without this file, core.autocrlf=true rewrote LF to CRLF on Windows checkouts
# while Linux CI kept LF, so the same artifact hashed two different ways
# depending on who looked at it. That is not hypothetical: mlb_model_promotion
# .json froze f110758c... (the CRLF rendering, captured on a Windows checkout)
# while every shadow lock in the ledger recorded f0177891... (the LF rendering,
# computed by CI). The gate's exact_cohort_required rule could therefore never
# be satisfied, and the MLB promotion gate spent weeks accumulating prospective
# evidence that its own policy would have rejected.
#
# Normalising in the working tree, not just in the object store, is the part
# that matters here -- `text` alone stores LF but still checks CRLF out.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Executed by cmd.exe, which is the one consumer that genuinely wants CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary payloads: never transform, never diff as text.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.gz binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
